THIS OUTPUT LOOKS WRONG AND IS RIGHT. DO NOT "FIX" IT.

A list, a nested list and a paragraph go in. A bullet and a CODE BLOCK come
out. That is CommonMark, not a defect, and the case exists to stop the next
reader from spending an afternoon on it.

WHY. A list item may begin with at most one blank line. `- ` followed by a
blank line is an item with no content, and an empty item cannot go on to hold
further blocks. So the list ENDS at line 1. The lines below it are no longer
inside any item, and from the left margin they are indented four columns,
which is an indented code block.

The four columns are the whole of it. Written under an item that HAS content,
the same lines are a nested list and a continuation, because content there
begins at column 2 and four is only two columns past it. It is the empty first
line that moves the goalposts to the margin.

WHAT THIS CASE IS GUARDING, THEN. That md keeps following cmark here rather
than guessing at what the author meant. The author probably did mean a nested
list, and md cannot act on that: the reference parser says code, every other
CommonMark tool will say code, and a document that md alone reads differently
is worse than one that is uniformly surprising.

Related, and genuinely a defect rather than a surprise: the bullet on line 1
used to vanish entirely, taking the list with it. See
`format-keeps-an-empty-list-item`. The `-` in the expected output is that fix.
